Day 280/300: Partitioning Into Minimum Number Of Deci-Binary Numbers

Problem Statement #1689

Problem Statement Explanation:

A decimal number is called deci-binary if each of its digits is either 0 or 1 without any leading zeros. 
For example, 101 and 1100 are deci-binary, while 112 and 3001 are not.
Given a string n that represents a positive decimal integer, return the minimum number of positive deci-binary numbers needed so that they sum up to n.

Solution Approach:

The core trick to this problem is understanding that a deci-binary number can only contribute a maximum value of 1 to any specific digit position (ones, tens, hundreds, etc.).
If the input string n contains a digit d at any position, you must have at least d deci-binary numbers to reach that sum for that specific column. For example:
If n=3, you need 1+1+1 (3 numbers).

If n=32, the '3' in the tens place requires three deci-binary numbers (like 10, 10, 10). 
The '2' in the ones place only requires two (like 1, 1, 0). The "bottleneck" is the largest digit.
Therefore, the answer is simply the maximum digit present in the string.

Initialize a variable maxDigit to 0.
Iterate through each character of the string n.
Convert the character to its integer value.
Update maxDigit if the current digit is larger.
Optimization: If maxDigit reaches 9, return immediately, as no digit can be higher than 9.
Return maxDigit.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public int minPartitions(String n) {
        int maxDigit = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n.length(); i++) {
            int currentDigit = n.charAt(i) - '0';
            if (currentDigit > maxDigit) {
                maxDigit = currentDigit;
            }
            if (maxDigit == 9) return 9;
        }
        return maxDigit;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time	Complexity: O(L)
Space Complexity: O(n)



